Chipalooza Analog Harness for IHP SG13CMOS5L
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Chipalooza Analog Harness design was created for the Open Circuit
Design "Chipalooza Challenge", a program to develop analog circuit blocks
for use in SoC designs, make them entirely open source and publicly
available, and make them roughly compatible across the three major open
source PDKs (Sky130, GF180MCU, and SG13CMOS5L).  Measurement and
characterization of the analog blocks requires a test structure, and to
minimize costs, an analog "harness" chip was developed to accommodate
multiple analog blocks with individual and shared resources including
pins, biases, and digital communication.

Efabless, Inc. ran the first Chipalooza challenge in 2024 on the Sky130
process, using a custom-made test chip based on the Efabless Caravel
"openframe" structure.  The Chipalooza harness chip in this repository
is based on the Caravel Openframe (in this case ported to IHP SG13CMOS5L),
but differs from the original, partly in pin count (80 padframe pins instead
of the original 64, although both designs go in a 64-pin package), and
mainly by having specific "slots" for users to place their analog blocks.
The fixed definition of the slots allows for the automation of the top-level
assembly of the test chip.

The idea behind the "harness" chip is that many circuit designers want to
spend time designing, simulating, testing, validating, and measuring a single
circuit or subsystem for a specific purpose.  Manufacturing and testing that
circuit requires an entire additional layer of skills, work, and time.  The
circuit under test needs significant infrastructure:  Control logic, padframe,
stable voltage and current biases, and an interface to get signals and data
off chip to a host computer and instrumentation.  The purpose of the harness
chip is to provide that infrastructure.

Unlike previous harness designs, the Chipalooza harness is designed such that
each user project has access to dedicated padframe pads, and these pads can
be any pad (from the same foundry I/O set) that the user wishes to have there.
This means that every aspect of the chip is customizable.  Chip top level
assembly requires filling out a configuration file "config.txt" in the top
level directory containing a list of pad types for each project.  Automated
scripts then assemble all of the chip views and layout based on templates
and the configuration file definitions.

This specific project is a three-level hierarchy:

(1) The padframe is a fixed-pin-count 80-pin design which is not based on the
    Efabless "caravel" but is a new design for the Chipalooza test chip
    series.  The size of the chip is fixed according to both the pin count and
    the size and number of spacers, all of which can be modified in the
    padframe template as needed.  

(2) The "chipalooza frame wrapper" fills the entire area inside the padframe,
    connecting to power and ground, and analog or general-purpose digital I/O.
    It contains the "housekeeping" SPI, registers, and sequencer, the
    sequencer SRAM, and the project slot power switches, shared current
    and voltage bias generators, and all other shared resources.

(3) A "slot" is an analog user project that fits in one of the eighteen
    avaiable locations in the chipalooza frame wrapper area.

The design flow is described here, with information about how to create an
analog project that fits into a slot, or to create an entirely new openframe
project.  All design steps are handled by scripts.

The openframe repository itself is designed to be as independent of a specific
PDK as possible, so it is relatively easy to port between processes.  Each
process will of course have its own I/O library with its own pad definitions,
which is mainly what drives the process-specific implementation.  While porting
is made convenient, it is not really possible/practical to create one repository
that is compatible with multiple processes.  So it is expected that an openframe
repository is process-specific; this one is for IHP SG13CMOS5L.

Unlike the original Efabless implementation, this openframe harness design aims
to have user-defined pad types in each signal pad position, as well as pre-
defined pad types for shared analog and digital resources.  Each foundry PDK has
different analog and digital cells in the I/O library with different inputs and
outputs.  The original openframe harness was for sky130, which had an unusually
complex general-purpose I/O design that was both analog and digital capable.
That made it convenient to design a single fixed padframe supporting both analog
and digital projects.  Since other PDKs don't have the combination I/O, it was
necessary to work out a method by which each signal pad can be selected.  But
that implies that the core-facing signals change when any pad changes, so a
large number of verilog, schematic, and layout files have to change accordingly,
which in turn requires a large amount of automation.  This repository provides
that automation.

These instructions are currently very incomplete.  The steps below require running
individual scripts.  The scripts will eventually be collected into a Makefile,
where each step is a process of "make <something>", and instructions will be
modified accordingly.

To create a new Chipalooza test chip with custom user analog projects:

(1)  Clone or fork this repository.  You probably want to create a new branch for
     the test chip;  then each test chip has the same upstream repository but
     exists in its own development branch.

(2)  All scripts make use of an environment variable PDK_ROOT which should be set
     appropriately to point to the directory containing the PDK (ihp-sg13cmos5l).

(3)  Run the script scripts/setup.sh to import IHP open PDK library views into
     local .mag files.  This is largely specific to the IHP PDK, which does not
     have .mag views for its standard cell and I/O libraries.  It creates two
     directories, magic/sg13cmos5l_stdcell and magic/sg13cmos5l_io, and populates
     them with .mag files, so that working in magic does not require reading the
     GDS of the PDK libraries each time.  These two directories are excluded from
     pushes to the upstream repository.

(4)  Edit the "config.txt" file.  This contains a "project_id" value which is an
     arbitrary hex string of 8 digits.  This corresponds to a metal text block on
     the layout, and a digital 32-bit ID value made available to the openframe
     project.  It is recommended that the last four bits of the code be reserved
     for a revision number.

     This is followed by a list of signal pads.  The signal pads are listed in
     counter-clockwise order of their physical position in the padframe.  This
     allows the openframe project designer to choose whether each of the signal
     pads will be analog, or digital input, output, or bidirectional.  The user
     does not need to supply any additional information about the pads.  The top
     level pin names will remain the same ("gpio[*]"), and the internal signal
     names will be derived from the pad signal names, with an index.

     The signal pad list entries are in the form:

		sN_an[x]:  pad_name

     where "N" is the project slot number, 1 to 16 (there are 18 total slots;
     the last two have no dedicated pads and so do not appear in the config
     file).

     Example:
		s1_an[0]: sg13cmos5l_IOPadInOut4mA

     Core signal names are derived from a signal name.  For the SG13CMOS5L
     technology, if the pad is a digital input-only or digital output-only pad,
     then there is only one core facing signal, which is what is given in the
     config.txt file.  If the pad is an analog pad, there are two signals, one
     of which is the pad name (that is, the core name = the pad name because
     there is a direct connection to the pad and the signals are the same), and
     the other of which is the given signal name.  If the pad is one of the
     "InOut" pads, then there are five core signals <signal_name>_in,
     <signal_name>_out, <signal_name>_ena, <signal_name>_one, and
     <signal_name>_zero.  The first three are the pad-to-core signal, the
     core-to-pad signal, and the output enable signal, respectively.  The
     additional two signals are digital zero and one outputs provided next to
     the pad so that a project can opt to tie off control signals at the pad, or
     to use these to power digital logic close to the pad.  In the example above,
     the core facing signals would be:

		s1_an_0_out
		s1_an_0_in
		s1_an_0_ena
		s1_an_0_one
		s1_an_0_zero

     Note that only the user anlog project dedicated pads can be reassigned.  The
     power pads, SPI pins, and the shared analog and digital pins are fixed.  Note
     that this does not prevent the user from specifying a power or ground pad in
     one or more of the project's dedicated pad locations;  the only restriction is
     that the location must be occupied by a wirebond pad, or else the wirebond
     diagram becomes invalid).

     To recap:  For SG13CMOS5L's PDK I/O library, there are eleven pad types, with
     pin and core names derived per the following example:

     1. Input-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadIn	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     2. Output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadOut4mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     3. Medium-drive output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadOut16mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     4. High-drive output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signal is:		mysig

     5. Tri-state output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadTriOut4mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig
					mysig_ena

     6. Medium-drive tri-state output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadTriOut16mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     7. High-drive tri-state output-only pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadTriOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     8. Bidrectional pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadInOut4mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig_in
					mysig_out
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one
     9. Medium-drive bidrectional pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadInOut16mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig_in
					mysig_out
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     10. High-drive bidrectional pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadInOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	mysig_in
					mysig_out
					mysig_ena
					mysig_zero
					mysig_one

     11. Analog pad:

	gpio[0]:	sg13cmos5l_IOPadInOut30mA	mysig

		pad signal is:		gpio[0]
		core signals are:	gpio[0]
					mysig_esd

	Note that the analog pad is slightly different from other pads:
	For all other pads, the user project wrapper pin names are the
	same as the padframe core-facing pin names.  However, the analog
	pad is connected directly to the core-facing non-ESD pin, so in
	the padframe, the core-facing pin name is the same as the pad name.
	In the user project wrapper, however, the core-facing non-ESD pin
	is given the name "mysig".

     Signals should not be indexed, as then different signals would have
     different index ranges which would not match the indexes of the pads.

     All pads in this library are the same size.

(5)  Run the script "scripts/parse_config.py".  This script reads in the
     configuration file and produces:

	1) A verilog netlist of the padframe in verilog/gl/sg13cmos5l_padframe.v
	2) A verilog netlist of the user project wrapper in
	   verilog/gl/openframe_project_wrapper.v
	3) A Tcl script used in the next step to generate the padframe and
	   wrapper layouts.

     If the script determines that any of the output files already exist, it
     will not run.  Delete the existing files before re-running the config
     file parser.

     WARNING:  Because the "chipalooza_frame_wrapper" layout and verilog
     contain the user project, MAKE SURE that you do not overwrite existing
     layout and netlist work.  (See comments below about creating the frame
     wrapper layout.)  No verilog netlist is made for the analog user project
     slots, which are assumed to be analog/mixed signal and exist as a schematic,
     not verilog.

(6)  Run the script "scripts/run_gen_padframe.sh".  This script reads in the
     configuration file and produces:

	1) A padframe layout in magic/sg13cmos5l_padframe.mag
	2) The user project wrapper layout in magic/openframe_project_wrapper.mag

     This script must be run from the magic/ directory.  If the script determines
     that a file "sg13cmos5_padframe.mag" exists, it will not run.  It may be run
     again at any time by deleting the padframe layout.

(7)  Run the script "set_user_id.py".  This script reads the configuration file
     and produces:

	1) A modified layout "user_id_textblock.mag" with the text of the project
           ID in metal
	2) A modified layout "user_id_vias.mag" with the via programming for the
           32-bit ROM matching the project ID.
	3) A modified "caravel_openframe.v" with the project ID set
	4) A modified "user_id_programming.v" with the project ID bits set

Now the openframe project is set up and ready for the project wrapper area to be
completed.  As mentioned above, the user project for an analog or mixed-signa chip
is generally done as a schematic.  The pinout of the user project itself does not
need to match the names in the wrapper.  The wrapper is the place where the user
project is instantiated and wired up to the wrapper pins.

(8)  If there are digital synthesized blocks inside the user project, then they
     should be run through LibreLane individually.  From the output of LibreLane,
     copy the verilog netlist from the flow after fill insertion (in the "standard"
     flow, this is 51-openroad-fillinsertion/).  Use the ".pnl.v" file, which is
     the one that has power pins defined on the standard cells, and is appropriate
     for LVS.  You will also want the ".nl.v" netlist for mixed-mode simulation.
     This works best if your verilog includes module files from a single file
     listing all of the includes.  Either keep a separate list file for each
     purpose (LVS or simulation), or use "`ifdef SIM" and/or "`ifdef LVS" in the
     file to choose the appropriate netlist.

     Copy the layout from the post-stream-out directory.  In the "standard"
     LibreLane flow, this is 55-magic-streamout-1.  The layout should be the
     ".mag" file, not the GDS, as the ".mag" file keeps references to the
     standard cell, not complete copies of the standard cells.

(9)  The user project top level works best as a separate level of hierarchy from
     the wrapper.  By convention, this is called "openframe_user_project" although
     it can be called anything.  These instructions will refer to the user project
     cell as "chipalooza_frame_wrapper".

     The tools that prepare the openframe will regenerate the wrapper files.
     Keeping the user project in a separate hierarchical level avoids the
     misfortune of accidentally deleting the entire user project.  In the
     worst case, the user project layout needs only to be placed inside the
     wrapper layout, and the user project module needs to be placed in the
     wrapper module with appropriate signal connections.  With the separated
     hierarchy, the file "openframe_project_wrapper.v" instances the module
     "openframe_user_project" (see above), which typically will not exist
     as a verilog module at all, but as a schematic.

(10) These instructions are not meant to cover how to create the user project
     other than mentioning how to pull results from LibreLane (see above)
     and how to connect the project to the wrapper.  As a general
     recommendation, however, the layout is best done by first dumping the
     openframe_project_wrapper layout contents into the openframe_user_project
     layout.  Pin labels can then be erased where they are not used, or
     renamed to match the user project pinout.  This will ensure that when
     the user project is later placed inside the wrapper, the pins will match
     exactly.  Do not edit the wrapper itself other than to place the user
     project inside it, as the pins and boundary are placed to exactly match
     to the padframe.

Once the user project itself is created and validated, the assembly into
the final chip goes as follows:

(11) Run the script "run_gen_wrapper_gds.sh".
     The script "run_gen_wrapper_gds.sh" creates GDS of the user project
     wrapper and its contents.  Note that as a custom analog layout,
     magic should generate GDS with hierarchical corrections, which is
     the default way that magic writes GDS.  Top level assembly is
     different;  because everything outside of the wrapper is a PDK
     library component (I/O cell or standard cell), no hierarchical
     processing is needed, which greatly increases the speed at which
     magic writes the GDS output.  "run_gen_wrapper_gds.sh" creates
     the GDS file which is then pulled into the final chip verbatim.
     The GDS file is "gds/openframe_project_wrapper.gds.gz".

(12) Run the script "run_gen_openframe_gds.sh".  This step assembles
     the user project wrapper GDS created in the previous step with
     the padframe and seal ring, generates a (pre-fill) top-level
     .mag file of the layout, and writes out a (pre-fill) top-level
     GDS file called "gds/sg13cmos5l_caravel_openframe.gds.gz".

(13) "cd" to the gds/ directory and run the script "generate_fill.py"
     as follows:

	../scripts/generate_fill.py sg13cmos5l_caravel_openframe.gds.gz -dist 

     This step generates the fill patterns for the chip, in a separate
     GDS file called sg13cmos5l_caravel_openframe_fill_pattern.gds.gz.

(14) Run the script "run_gen_filled_final_gds.sh" to get the (post-fill)
     final design.  The final top level has its own cell name.  By default
     (which is not an IHP-approved name) the top level cell will be
     called "sg13cmos5l_caravel_openframe_final".  For IHP submissions, IHP
     will provide the name of the top level cell as part of the submission
     process.  To get the correct final top level cell name and file name,
     set the environment variable "PROJECT" to the cell name before running
     the script.

(15) Final DRC verification:  The script "run_klayout_drc_final.sh" runs
     klayout DRC on the final design GDS.  If environment PROJECT has been
     set (see above), then the final DRC result can be found in
     validate/${PROJECT}.lyrdb

(16) Final LVS verification:  The script "run_extract_openframe.sh" does a
     full layout extraction on the top level, producing a netlist that
     is placed in netlist/layout/.  If the PROJECT environment variable
     has been set, then the final netlist is netlist/layout/${PROJECT}.spice.

(17) Final LVS:  The script "run_lvs_openframe.sh" runs top-level LVS.
     Note that since the LVS process is highly dependent on what exists
     in the user project area, this script needs to be modified for each
     project.  It is normally set up to start with the top level verilog
     and read the user project from a netlist, which should be extracted
     from the user project schematic.  Everything above the user project
     is in a verilog netlist.  If the top level cell has been named
     according to environment variable PROJECT, then a top level verilog
     netlist needs to be made for a module with the name ${PROJECT}.  This
     is easy to do by copying the "sg13cmos5l_caravel_openframe_final.v"
     file to ${PROJECT}.v and changing the module name to ${PROJECT}.

     [To do:  Currently top level LVS through magic and netgen fails due
     to issues in the padframe I/O netlists in the PDK, which need to be
     resolved.  Checking LVS with klayout has not been attempted and would
     require converting the verilog of the padframe, wrapper, and top
     level cell to SPICE/CDL]
     
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Mixed-signal multi-project mixed-signal harness
-------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository contains a full example of an openframe design in the form of a
multi-project mixed-signal harness design.  This is a framework for aggregating
up to eighteen analog or mixed-signal sub-projects, each one able to be separately
powered.

The user project has dependent libraries which are hierarchical and therefore also
independent git projects.  They should be instantiated as git submodules.  The
submodules can be cloned with the project by doing

git clone https://github.com/RTimothyEdwards/sg13cmos5l_ocd_ip__openframe.git \
	--recurse-submodules

or after cloning, by doing

git submodule update --init --recursive

That initializes the dependent repositories used for the project example.  For a
different project, new submodules can be added by first creating the IP as a git
project, use

git submodule add <repository_URL>

For simplicity, keep the subdirectory name the same as the repository.  After
adding the submodule, commit and push as usual.

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Mixed-signal sub-projects
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The mixed-signal aggregator has not yet formalized the areas available for analog
and mixed-signal projects.  The idea is that the empty area of the chip is divided
into eighteen slots.  Each slot has an identical footprint.  It gets its 3.3V power
and 1.2V power supplies through a pMOS power switch, and is given pins to connect
to the digital interface of the harness (control and status lines), regulated
voltage bias signals, regulated current bias signals, and shared analog lines.
